  "notes": [
    "First pain and second pain: a sharp, well-located sting carried by thinly myelinated Aδ fibers (about 5–30 m/s), then a duller, burning, longer pain carried by unmyelinated C fibers (about 0.4–2 m/s) (MSU open textbook Introduction to Neuroscience, \"Pain\"; Wikipedia, \"Nociceptor\" and \"Group C nerve fiber\").",
    "About a meter of nerve from the toe to the spinal cord in an adult is a rough estimate (the leg is about 90 cm and the cord ends in the lower back); it depends on height. At 25 m/s that is about 0.04 s; at 1 m/s about 1 s.",
    "The textbook example is hitting your thumb with a hammer: \"an intense, sharp pain – first pain, but then a milder, burning or aching pain that continue longer – second pain\" (University of Iowa pressbook, \"Pain\").",
    "The chart is a sketch: the shape and the gap are illustrative, not measured.",
    "Simplified drawing: one fiber of each kind, one relay neuron. Real nerves carry thousands of fibers.",
    "Educational only, no first aid or treatment advice. If a pain or an injury worries you, see a professional."
  ],
